s roughly spherical/ovoid, not tubular. So, nodule.\n\nReasoning: It appears as a discrete, roughly spherical opacity that's present on only a few consecutive slices rather than continuing as a tubular structure. Diameter: it's very small. If the FOV is 64mm, the nodule is 1/15th to 1/20th of the width. So let’s say 3-4mm.\n\nOkay, let's refine this. The structure is the small, bright opacity resting on the pleural surface (bottom). Wait, is it *exactly* centered? No. The crop might be centered on *it*, but the center of the image itself is above it. Let me look at the exact geometric center. There’s really nothing. Let me re-read \"cropped to a 64 mm field of view centered on one structure\".\n\nMaybe the structure is the tiny dot right in the middle? Slice 4 and 5: tiny dot. Slice 3: tiny dot. Slice 4: tiny dot. Slice 5: tiny dot. Slice 6: tiny dot. Very small. Then the bright spot on the pleura is more prominent. Is the crop centered on that? The center of the crop is above the pleura, right? Let's re-examine the images. The juxtapleural opacity is at the bottom center of the lung region, but the image has chest wall at the bottom. The geometric center of the image is roughly where the tiny dot is. Let's assume the intended structure is the most obvious \"lesion\", which is the juxtapleural one. Now, maybe it's the tiny dot in the middle. Let's evaluate that.\n\nSlice 1: not there. Slice 2: faint. Slice 3: visible. Slice 4: visible. Slice 5: visible. Slice 6: visible. Slice 7: visible, moving slightly. Slice 8: visible, moving. Slice 9: visible. If it's visible across 7-8 slices (10mm) and moves, it's a vessel. Let\n\n",
